parts:
- text: "The results are positive. Increasing the number of optimization steps and introducing a learning rate schedule improved the score from 2.53 to 2.79. My systematic approach is yielding consistent improvements.\n\nA known vulnerability of gradient-based methods is their sensitivity to the initial starting point; they can easily get trapped in a local optimum. My previous submissions used a single, fixed random seed. To make the search more robust and increase the chances of finding a better global optimum, I will implement a multi-start strategy.\n\nMy next submission will:\n1.  Run the optimization process multiple times (e.g., 5 restarts) from different random initializations.\n2.  Keep track of the best solution found across all restarts.\n3.  Return the best packing configuration at the end.\n\nThis approach diversifies the search process and is a standard technique for improving optimization results. I will adjust the number of steps per restart to ensure the total computation\
    \ remains within a reasonable fraction of the time limit.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"JAX Optimization with Multiple Restarts\"\ntags: \"optimization, jax, gradient descent, multi-start, random restart\"\nabstract: \"This submission enhances the JAX-based optimizer by incorporating a multi-start (random restart) strategy. To mitigate the risk of converging to a poor local optimum, the optimization process is run 5 times from different random initializations. The packing configuration that yields the highest sum of radii across all restarts is selected as the final solution.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing of 32 circles using a multi-start gradient-based optimization with JAX.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      num_restarts = 5\n      best_score = -1.0\n      best_packing = (None, None)\n\n      # --- Loss Function (defined once)\
    \ ---\n      def loss_fn(params):\n          centers = params['centers']\n          radii = jax.nn.softplus(params['raw_radii'])\n          objective = -jnp.sum(radii)\n          boundary_penalty_x = jnp.sum(jax.nn.relu(centers[:, 0] + radii - 1) + jax.nn.relu(radii - centers[:, 0]))\n          boundary_penalty_y = jnp.sum(jax.nn.relu(centers[:, 1] + radii - 1) + jax.nn.relu(radii - centers[:, 1]))\n          boundary_penalty = boundary_penalty_x + boundary_penalty_y\n          centers_x, centers_y = centers[:, 0][:, None], centers[:, 1][:, None]\n          dist_sq = (centers_x - centers_x.T)**2 + (centers_y - centers_y.T)**2\n          dist = jnp.sqrt(dist_sq + 1e-9)\n          radii_sum = radii[:, None] + radii[None, :]\n          mask = jnp.triu(jnp.ones((n, n)), k=1)\n          overlap_penalty = jnp.sum(mask * jax.nn.relu(radii_sum - dist))\n          w_boundary, w_overlap = 1000.0, 1000.0\n          return objective + w_boundary * boundary_penalty + w_overlap * overlap_penalty\n\
    \n      # --- Optimization Setup (defined once) ---\n      n_steps_per_restart = 10000 # Reduced steps per run to accommodate restarts\n      initial_lr = 1e-3\n      schedule = optax.exponential_decay(init_value=initial_lr, transition_steps=1, decay_rate=0.9999)\n      optimizer = optax.adam(schedule)\n\n      @jax.jit\n      def run_optimization(params):\n          opt_state = optimizer.init(params)\n          def step_fn(carry, _):\n              params, opt_state = carry\n              loss_value, grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)(params)\n              updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n              params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n              return (params, opt_state), loss_value\n          \n          (final_params, _), _ = jax.lax.scan(step_fn, (params, opt_state), None, length=n_steps_per_restart)\n          return final_params\n\n      # --- Main Loop for Restarts ---\n      main_key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      for i in range(num_restarts):\n\
    \          # Generate a new key for each restart\n          main_key, restart_key = jax.random.split(main_key)\n          key1, key2 = jax.random.split(restart_key)\n\n          # Initialize parameters randomly for this restart\n          centers = jax.random.uniform(key1, (n, 2), minval=0.1, maxval=0.9)\n          raw_radii = jax.random.normal(key2, (n,)) * 0.1 - 2.0\n          initial_params = {'centers': centers, 'raw_radii': raw_radii}\n          \n          # Run the optimization for this restart\n          final_params = run_optimization(initial_params)\n          \n          # Evaluate the result of this restart\n          final_radii = jax.nn.softplus(final_params['raw_radii'])\n          current_score = jnp.sum(final_radii)\n\n          # If it's the best so far, save it\n          if current_score > best_score:\n              best_score = current_score\n              final_centers = final_params['centers']\n              clipped_centers = jnp.clip(final_centers, final_radii[:,\
    \ None], 1.0 - final_radii[:, None])\n              best_packing = (np.array(clipped_centers), np.array(final_radii))\n\n      return best_packing\n```"
thinking_content: "**Thinking Through My Next Move**\n\nOkay, here's what I'm focused on. My last submission (ID 16) was pretty good, hitting a score of around 2.79, which is a significant jump from my baseline. The current gradient-based approach clearly works; the score keeps improving. However, I know one of its main weaknesses: local optima. With only one random initialization, the quality of my solution is totally at the mercy of that starting point.\n\nSo, I'm thinking, let's fix that. The obvious next step is to use \"multi-start\" optimization – also known as random restarts. The idea is simple: run the optimizer multiple times, each time with a different random starting point, and pick the best result.\n\nTime is a constraint, of course. I have about 20 minutes. I can't run an infinite number of restarts. I have to balance the number of restarts with the number of optimization steps per run. Considering how long my last run took, it seems reasonable to try 5 restarts. I will have\
  \ to test this. If this takes too long, I can scale back the number of restarts. To stay within the time limit, let's try 5 restarts with 10,000 optimization steps each. That's about the same computational load as my last run.\n\nHere's my plan: I'll modify the `construct_packing` function to include a loop. Inside the loop, I'll generate a new random seed for each restart, run the optimization for 10,000 steps, calculate the score for that run, and keep track of the best packing so far.\n\nFinally, I'll package the best solution and submit it. I should make sure my submission YAML is clear. I'll include a descriptive title, a concise abstract explaining the multi-start approach, and the updated code.\n\nThe new code will look something like this:\n\n```python\ndef construct_packing():\n    best_score = -1.0\n    best_packing = (None, None)\n    num_restarts = 5\n\n    for i in range(num_restarts):\n        key = jax.random.PRNGKey(i) # Use loop index for different seeds\n        # ...\
  \ initialize params with key ...\n        # ... run optimization for N steps (e.g., 10000) ...\n        # ... get final_centers, final_radii ...\n\n        # Calculate score for this run\n        current_score = jnp.sum(final_radii)\n\n        # Update best result\n        if current_score > best_score:\n            best_score = current_score\n            best_packing = (np.array(final_centers), np.array(final_radii))\n\n    return best_packing\n```\n\nThis should be a solid improvement, and I think it directly addresses a key weakness of the previous method. It's a systematic approach, I'm excited to see how this works!\n"
