tick: 14
role: model
parts:
- text: "This is an interesting and important result. The multi-start strategy resulted in a lower score (2.72) compared to the single, long run (2.79). This is a crucial finding.\n\n**Analysis:**\nMy hypothesis was that multiple restarts would help avoid local optima. However, to accommodate the restarts, I reduced the number of optimization steps per run from 50,000 to 10,000. The decrease in score strongly suggests that the **depth of convergence in a single run is more critical than the breadth of searching from multiple starting points**, at least with the current hyperparameters. The 10,000 steps were likely insufficient for the optimizer to find a high-quality solution in any of the individual runs.\n\n**Next Step:**\nBased on this analysis, I will revert to the single-run approach that previously yielded the best score. My new hypothesis is that an even longer optimization run will allow for deeper convergence and thus a better score. I will test this by significantly increasing\
    \ the number of optimization steps. I will triple the steps from 50,000 to 150,000, as the previous runs were completed well within the time limit.\n\nThis is a direct, systematic test of the convergence-depth hypothesis.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"JAX Optimization with Extended Convergence Time\"\ntags: \"optimization, jax, gradient descent, convergence, long run\"\nabstract: \"Building on the successful single-run optimizer (ID 16), this submission tests the hypothesis that deeper convergence leads to better scores. The number of optimization steps is tripled from 50,000 to 150,000 to allow the Adam optimizer significantly more time to refine the circle packing configuration.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      \"\"\"\n      Constructs a packing of 32 circles using a single, extended gradient-based optimization run.\n      \"\"\"\n      n = 32\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\
    \ # Re-using the best seed so far\n\n      # Initialize parameters randomly\n      key, subkey1, subkey2 = jax.random.split(key, 3)\n      centers = jax.random.uniform(subkey1, (n, 2), minval=0.1, maxval=0.9)\n      raw_radii = jax.random.normal(subkey2, (n,)) * 0.1 - 2.0\n\n      params = {'centers': centers, 'raw_radii': raw_radii}\n\n      # --- Loss Function (same as before) ---\n      def loss_fn(params):\n          centers = params['centers']\n          radii = jax.nn.softplus(params['raw_radii'])\n\n          objective = -jnp.sum(radii)\n\n          boundary_penalty_x = jnp.sum(jax.nn.relu(centers[:, 0] + radii - 1) + jax.nn.relu(radii - centers[:, 0]))\n          boundary_penalty_y = jnp.sum(jax.nn.relu(centers[:, 1] + radii - 1) + jax.nn.relu(radii - centers[:, 1]))\n          boundary_penalty = boundary_penalty_x + boundary_penalty_y\n\n          centers_x = centers[:, 0][:, None]\n          centers_y = centers[:, 1][:, None]\n          dist_sq = (centers_x - centers_x.T)**2\
    \ + (centers_y - centers_y.T)**2\n          dist = jnp.sqrt(dist_sq + 1e-9)\n\n          radii_sum = radii[:, None] + radii[None, :]\n          mask = jnp.triu(jnp.ones((n, n)), k=1)\n          \n          overlap_penalty = jnp.sum(mask * jax.nn.relu(radii_sum - dist))\n\n          w_boundary = 1000.0\n          w_overlap = 1000.0\n          total_loss = objective + w_boundary * boundary_penalty + w_overlap * overlap_penalty\n\n          return total_loss\n\n      # --- Optimization (Extended) ---\n      n_steps = 150000 # Increased number of steps significantly\n      \n      # Learning rate schedule: exponential decay\n      initial_lr = 1e-3\n      decay_rate = 0.9999\n      schedule = optax.exponential_decay(\n          init_value=initial_lr,\n          transition_steps=1,\n          decay_rate=decay_rate\n      )\n      \n      optimizer = optax.adam(schedule)\n      opt_state = optimizer.init(params)\n\n      @jax.jit\n      def step(params, opt_state):\n          loss_value, grads\
    \ = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)(params)\n          updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n          params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n          return params, opt_state, loss_value\n\n      for i in range(n_steps):\n          params, opt_state, loss_value = step(params, opt_state)\n      \n      # --- Final Result ---\n      final_centers = params['centers']\n      final_radii = jax.nn.softplus(params['raw_radii'])\n      \n      final_centers = jnp.clip(final_centers, final_radii[:, None], 1.0 - final_radii[:, None])\n\n      return np.array(final_centers), np.array(final_radii)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Deep Dive on Convergence: Building on Success, Analyzing Failure**


  Okay, so here''s where my thinking is at.  The last experiment with multi-restarts? The score dropped, which is valuable intel. It wasn''t the winning ticket, but it gave me a lot to chew on. I had this hunch that spreading the optimization across multiple starting points would give me a better chance of finding a global minimum. But, the results are pointing me in another direction, leading me to focus on getting a much deeper convergence.


  The key observation is that the score went down, not up. That suggests the 10,000 steps per restart just wasn''t enough time for each optimization to find a really good solution. I''m now thinking that the "depth" of a single, long run is more important at this stage than casting a wider net with multiple shorter runs. The fact that the single, 50,000-step run gave me the best result so far really confirms this.


  Therefore, the next logical step is to really push the "depth" idea. I''m going to go back to a single run but significantly increase the number of steps. I''ll triple it from 50,000 to 150,000 steps. This is a very direct test of the hypothesis that more convergence time leads to a better score. My persona as a systematic experimenter loves this plan. It''s a clean, controlled test, building directly on the previous successful method.


  To do this, I''ll take the code from submission ID 16, which got the 2.79 score. Then, I''ll change the `n_steps` from 50000 to 150000. I''ll make sure to update the title to something like "JAX Optimization with Extended Convergence Time", and I''ll keep the relevant tags like "optimization", "jax", "gradient descent", and "learning rate schedule", and maybe add "convergence", and "long run." The abstract will clearly mention that this run is building on the previous successful one by tripling the number of optimization steps to explore the limits of convergence.


  Finally, I''ll prepare the submission command with the new YAML block. Hopefully, this one will take me closer to the optimal solution.

  '
