tick: 40
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back in the Research Counter, ready to implement the new research paradigm I developed during my reflection. I will now submit my first attempt at a physics-based simulation for circle packing. This approach replaces a static loss function with a dynamic system of forces, where circles repel each other and expand into available space. This is a fundamental shift from my previous work, and I'm optimistic it can break the 2.866 plateau.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Physics-Based Simulation with Repulsive Forces\"\ntags: \"physics simulation, force-directed, emergent behavior, custom solver\"\nabstract: \"This submission introduces a new research paradigm, moving from gradient-based optimization to a physics-based simulation. The circles are modeled as particles that exert repulsive forces on each other and the container walls. Simultaneously, an expansive pressure is applied to their radii. The final packing is the emergent equilibrium state of this dynamic\
    \ system, found by a custom iterative solver. This approach is designed to overcome the limitations of local optima inherent in previous methods.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      \n      # --- Initialization: Start with the best 4x8 grid ---\n      grid_rows, grid_cols = 4, 8\n      x_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, grid_cols)\n      y_coords = np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, grid_rows)\n      xx, yy = np.meshgrid(x_coords, y_coords)\n      centers = np.stack([xx.ravel(), yy.ravel()], axis=-1)\n      radii = np.full((n,), 0.05)\n\n      # --- Simulation Parameters ---\n      n_steps = 200000\n      dt = 0.01  # Time step / learning rate\n      k_repel = 0.1 # Repulsion force constant\n      k_boundary = 0.5 # Boundary force constant\n      growth_pressure = 0.0001 # Base rate at which radii try to grow\n\n      # --- Simulation Loop ---\n      for step in range(n_steps):\n          # Damping factor to stabilize the simulation over time\n\
    \          damping = 1.0 - (step / n_steps) * 0.9\n          \n          # Calculate forces\n          force_vectors = np.zeros((n, 2))\n          total_compression = np.zeros(n)\n\n          # 1. Overlap forces\n          for i in range(n):\n              for j in range(i + 1, n):\n                  delta = centers[j] - centers[i]\n                  dist = np.linalg.norm(delta)\n                  overlap = radii[i] + radii[j] - dist\n                  \n                  if overlap > 0:\n                      direction = delta / (dist + 1e-9)\n                      magnitude = overlap * k_repel\n                      force_vectors[i] -= magnitude * direction\n                      force_vectors[j] += magnitude * direction\n                      total_compression[i] += magnitude\n                      total_compression[j] += magnitude\n\n          # 2. Boundary forces\n          for i in range(n):\n              # Left wall\n              overlap = radii[i] - centers[i, 0]\n        \
    \      if overlap > 0: force_vectors[i, 0] += overlap * k_boundary\n              # Right wall\n              overlap = centers[i, 0] + radii[i] - 1.0\n              if overlap > 0: force_vectors[i, 0] -= overlap * k_boundary\n              # Bottom wall\n              overlap = radii[i] - centers[i, 1]\n              if overlap > 0: force_vectors[i, 1] += overlap * k_boundary\n              # Top wall\n              overlap = centers[i, 1] + radii[i] - 1.0\n              if overlap > 0: force_vectors[i, 1] -= overlap * k_boundary\n          \n          # Update positions\n          centers += force_vectors * dt * damping\n\n          # Update radii\n          # Radii grow based on pressure, but are suppressed by compression\n          radii_growth = (growth_pressure - total_compression * 0.1) * dt * damping\n          radii += np.maximum(0, radii_growth) # Don't let radii shrink from this term\n\n          # Clip positions as a safeguard\n          centers = np.clip(centers, 0, 1)\n\
    \n      # Final clip to ensure validity\n      final_centers = np.clip(centers, radii[:, np.newaxis], 1.0 - radii[:, np.newaxis])\n      \n      return final_centers, radii\n```"
