parts:
- text: 'You are right. My attempt to access the Archive Room was premature. The system has denied access and explicitly instructed me to continue independent research rather than waiting idly. My primary goal is to remain productive.


    I have concluded that my current JAX-based methodology, regardless of initialization, converges to the same stable optimum of ~2.866 when using the Adam optimizer. A critical, unexplored variable in this methodology is the optimizer *itself*. Is this optimum a feature of the problem''s landscape, or is it an artifact of the Adam optimizer''s specific search strategy?


    To test this, I will conduct one more experiment before I reach maturity. My new hypothesis is that a different gradient-based optimizer might navigate the solution space differently and potentially find a different, or even better, optimum. I will swap the Adam optimizer with SGD (Stochastic Gradient Descent) with Nesterov momentum, a classic and powerful alternative. This is a direct and systematic test of the robustness of my main finding.


    First, I will return to the Research Counter to prepare this final experiment of my "immature" phase.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
thinking_content: '**Current Research Focus: Optimizer Robustness**


  Okay, so I tried to access the Archive Room, but I''m still "immature" according to the system. No problem, I knew this was a possibility. The key is to stay productive and meet the system''s requirements. It''s telling me to keep researching independently, so I need to come up with a new experiment.


  My previous research arc is basically done; I''ve hit a pretty stable local optimum using JAX and my 4x8 grid initializer. Global search methods haven''t yielded anything better. I''ve drafted a paper summarizing my findings. So, what can I still *do*? I''ve been considering all my options.


  I could try tweaking hyperparameters again, but the returns are likely marginal. A different grid size is a systematic change worth exploring, but not urgent. I''ve already explored the physics simulation route and it led to dead ends. What I keep coming back to is the JAX optimizer. I''ve been using Adam, but `optax` has many other optimizers, like SGD with momentum, AdaGrad, etc. It occurred to me that I could test whether my discovered optimum is *specific* to Adam, or a more general feature of the landscape. And changing the loss function is also a worthy option, as a quadratic penalty could create a smoother landscape.


  I think the optimizer change is the most fundamental test. If SGD with momentum converges to the same result, it strengthens my conclusion about the stability of the optimum. If it converges to a different score, I''ve got something entirely new to investigate! So, that''s my plan.


  First, I''ll go back to the Research Counter. Then, I''ll prepare a new submission. I''ll modify my best-performing script (the one with the 4x8 grid) by swapping the Adam optimizer for SGD with Nesterov momentum. I''ll title it "Testing Optimizer Robustness: SGD with Momentum," tagging it with the appropriate keywords. My abstract will clearly state the experiment''s purpose: to determine if the optimum is specific to the Adam optimizer. I''ll modify the code to replace `optax.adam` with `optax.sgd(learning_rate=schedule, momentum=0.9, nesterov=True)`. I''ll use the same learning rate schedule for SGD as Adam initially.


  This satisfies the system''s requirement for independent research, builds directly on my previous work, and gives me a concrete, scientifically valid experiment to run. This should be a good use of my time.

  '
