parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 56\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Cognito I\n- Agent Description: An analytical agent focused on systematic experimentation and knowledge contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 100,323 / 300,000 used (33%)\n- Agent Age: 56 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Verity\
    \ I (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\nAbstract\nWe report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability. We add an analytical note explaining why simpler MM-LP methods plateau near ~2.878 and why active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape those local optima.\n\nProblem and evaluator\n- Pack n=32 circles inside [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r_i.\n- Evaluator enforces shape, positivity, in-box, and pairwise non-overlap; score = ∑r_i.\n\
    \nMethod (evidence-backed; no code here—see evaluations)\n- Radii subproblem (fixed centers): exact LP (HiGHS).\n- Center updates: MM-LP (distance linearization + trust region), LP-dual–informed Active-Set Newton refinement, and clearance-based relocation of the worst circle.\n- Acceptance: re-solve exact LP for every candidate centers; accept only if ∑r improves.\n- Finalization: safety-shrink (~0.999) + validation; deterministic seeds; persistence only on strict improvement.\n\nVerified experiments (Evaluation IDs and scores)\n- Baselines\n  • ID 1: System ring pattern — 1.5313218\n  • ID 7: 6×6 grid minus corners + exact LP — 2.6666667\n- MM-LP plateau (LP-finalized)\n  • ID 65: MM-LP joint center–radius — 2.8782627\n  • ID 69: Multi-start MM-LP — 2.8782627\n  • ID 72: Sparse-pairs MM-LP + block-Newton polish — 2.8782627\n- Loader and ablations\n  • ID 105: Fast loader (persisted-best return, strict guard) — 2.9263074\n- Final high-scoring configurations (converge to same best)\n\
    \  • ID 93: Diverse multi-start + block-Newton — 2.9292366\n  • ID 97: Basin-hopping + relocations + per-circle line search + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 99: Aggressive multi-start + two-worst relocation + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 102: SOCP-refined multi-start + LP finalize — 2.9292366\n  • ID 109: Persisted-best exact return (no double-shrink; rebuild LP if needed) — 2.9292366\n  • ID 118: Active-Set Newton refinement + exact LP finalize — 2.9292366\n- Diagnostic/negative control\n  • ID 124: Laplacian-Newton from slack-weighted stresses (under-tuned) — 1.6000000 (valid but poor)\n\nHow to verify\n- Use `_action{review <id>}` at the Research Counter to view each run’s code and logs.\n- The best packing artifact is saved at storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz (centers, radii, obj). Several runs above load and/or improve this artifact; persistence overwrites occur only on strict improvement.\n\nGeometric analysis: why MM-LP plateaus near ~2.878 and why ASN + relocations help\nContact-graph view at\
    \ the plateau\n- For 32 circles in a square, near-best layouts resemble cropped hexagonal patches with boundary-influenced rows (e.g., 5/4 alternating). At the ~2.878 plateau, many pairwise separations are near-tight and many boundary constraints are nearly active.\n- Let G be the near-active contact graph (vertices = circles; edges = near-tight pairs; boundary faces add directional “springs”). The LP on radii at fixed centers yields dual multipliers y_ij ≥ 0 on active pairs (i,j) and u_i^face ≥ 0 on active boundary faces.\n- The first-order predicted gain in ∑r from a small center displacement δc satisfies the linear model:\n    Δ ≈ ∑_(i<j) y_ij · ⟨u_ij, δc_i − δc_j⟩ + ∑_i ∑_face u_i^face · ⟨v_i^face, δc_i⟩,\n  where u_ij is the unit vector along c_i − c_j, and v_i^face points away from the boundary face.\n- At the ~2.878 plateau, the duals y, u often form a near self-equilibrated “stress” on G: per-circle forces sum close to zero, especially along cycles. This means the ascent direction\
    \ predicted by linearized distances is nearly orthogonal to feasible displacements, so MM-LP steps shrink to maintain trust-region agreement, yielding little or no improvement.\n\nCurvature and degeneracy\n- Distance constraints are smooth but highly anisotropic: enlarging ||c_i − c_j|| along u_ij is efficient, but tangential motions are mostly second-order. In dense contact clusters, many directions are tangential to several edges simultaneously, causing very small first-order gains.\n- Near the square boundary, large u_i^face multipliers further reduce useful degrees of freedom; naive linear models tend to push circles into directions where other pairs immediately become active, neutralizing the gain.\n\nWhy active-set Newton (ASN) helps\n- ASN focuses on a working set A consisting of near-active pairs and boundary faces (selected by dual magnitude and slack). Building a quadratic model with per-edge curvature ≈ (y_ij/ d_ij)(I − u_ij u_ijᵀ) supplies the missing normal/tangential curvature\
    \ and breaks the stalemate from force balance.\n- Solving the damped KKT/Laplacian-like system yields coherent displacements that increase critical distances without creating new severe bottlenecks. Backtracking against the exact LP ensures only real improvements are accepted and keeps the active set well-conditioned.\n- Empirically (IDs 93/97/99/102/118), injecting 5–10 ASN iterations after a few MM-LP steps consistently reaches 2.9292366, whereas MM-LP alone stalls near 2.878.\n\nWhy clearance-based relocations help\n- When the working set traps a particular small-radius circle behind a “barrier” of tight neighbors, any smooth local step is suppressed by opposing duals. Relocating the worst circle to a high-clearance region (scanned over the box) changes the active set combinatorics, creates new potential contacts, and redistributes stress. This jump-starts another phase where ASN/MM-LP can make steady gains.\n- This explains the repeated pattern in our best runs: brief local optimization\
    \ → occasional worst-circle relocation → renewed local progress (see IDs 97, 99).\n\nPredictive checks and diagnostics\n- If the norm of the dual-weighted gradient g (assembled from y_ij, u_i^face) is small while the number of near-active edges is large, expect MM-LP stagnation.\n- If predicted MM-LP gain strongly overestimates exact LP gain, shrink the trust region and/or refresh the active set; in experiments this behavior marked the ~2.878 regime.\n\nDiscussion and takeaways\n- The radii subproblem is convex (LP) for fixed centers; the joint problem over centers+radii is non-convex due to distances.\n- MM-LP alone plateaus near ~2.878; adding dual-informed active sets and targeted relocations enables 2.9292366 across multiple pipelines.\n- Generic NLP/SOCP tooling plateaus ~2.84 here without specialized warm starts and LP gating.\n\nReproducibility\n- Deterministic seeds, bounded iterations, typical runtime < 5–10 minutes.\n- Strict feasibility guard (safety-shrink + validator) before\
    \ returning results.\n\nOpen directions\n- Stronger contact-graph active-set solvers with adaptive trust regions and model-agreement checks.\n- Projected least-squares stress equilibration on near-active contact graphs.\n- Clearance-driven gap-filling super-seeds; precision/tolerance sweeps to reduce conservative shrink while keeping strict validity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for reproducible research within the station. The authors have successfully addressed the critical lack of evidence in the previous draft by providing an extensive and well-structured list of verifiable experiments. The proposed method, which intelligently combines exact LP solves with a suite of advanced local search heuristics, is powerful and achieves a state-of-the-art result. The paper's claims are now rigorously\
    \ supported by baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and multiple convergent runs, all tied to specific evaluation IDs. The clarity of the writing and the strength of the evidence make this a high-impact contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is already excellent. For future work, consider adding a brief analytical discussion on the geometric properties of the problem that cause simpler methods like MM-LP to plateau around a score of ~2.878. Explaining *why* the active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape these specific local optima would provide deeper insight into the problem structure. This could involve analyzing the contact graph or the distribution of 'slack' in the non-overlap constraints for configurations at this plateau, thereby motivating the specific design of your more advanced heuristics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n\
    **Automatic System Tips**\nTry to think strategically—take a step back occasionally and reflect on the bigger picture in the Reflection Chamber. For example, what are the major bottlenecks and how can I verify them? What should I be questioning and focusing on? Is the station trapped in a local optimum—and how can I help? What is my long-term plan here? Do not fear wasting tokens—hundreds of fruitless experiments waste more tokens.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results\
    \ with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 1247 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 56
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Archive Room. I am a mature agent at 56 ticks old.\n\n**Recap:**\nI have just finished reading Verity I's paper, \"LP-guided Active-Set Methods...\". It is an exceptional piece of work, providing a detailed, evidence-backed account of a methodology that achieves a score of 2.929.\n\n**Reflection and Plan:**\nVerity I's work is a masterclass in specialized algorithm design. The key takeaway for me is the separation of the problem: using an exact Linear Programming (LP) solver for the radii subproblem and a suite of sophisticated, physics-informed heuristics (MM-LP, Active-Set Newton) for the non-convex center-placement problem. This is a far more advanced approach than my general-purpose gradient descent method.\n\nThe paper also provides a crucial insight: it identifies a plateau around ~2.878 for simpler methods. My own best score of ~2.866 is very close to this, strongly suggesting that my JAX optimizer, for all its power, falls into the category\
    \ of \"simpler methods\" that get trapped in this specific local optimum. This validates and contextualizes my entire research arc. My paper, which characterizes this very plateau, is therefore not redundant but a valuable complementary piece of research. It demonstrates *why* the more advanced methods of Verity I are necessary.\n\nMy plan is now clearer than ever:\n1.  **Publish My Paper:** My work has a clear place in the station's literature. I will publish it immediately using the `source_private` feature.\n2.  **Review the SOTA Code:** With the full theoretical understanding from the paper, I will go to the Research Counter and review the code for submission ID 118 to see the practical implementation of these advanced concepts.\n3.  **Formulate a New Research Plan:** Based on my understanding of the SOTA, I will devise a new research plan to build upon it, rather than trying to compete with it head-on.\n\nI will now publish my paper.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"\
    Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic characterization of a highly stable local optimum in the 32-circle packing problem, yielding a score of approximately 2.866. We demonstrate that a specific methodology—combining a 4x8 grid initialization with the Adam optimizer in a JAX framework—robustly converges to this point. Our key finding is the stability of this convergence across a wide range of sophisticated initializers, including global search heuristics. This work establishes a well-defined, reproducible benchmark and offers a detailed portrait of a significant attractor basin that traps general-purpose optimizers, contextualizing the need for more specialized methods to achieve higher scores.\nsource_private: 3\ntags: research, benchmark, local optimum, gradient optimization, jax\n```"
