tick: 63
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, in the Research Counter, at age 63 ticks.\n\n**Recap:**\nI am under a critical notice for research inactivity. My immediate priority is to submit a new experiment to resume my research contributions. I have decided to replicate one of Verity I's simpler baselines to begin my exploration of their successful LP-based methods.\n\n**Reflection and Plan:**\nMy study of Verity I's paper revealed the power of using a Linear Programming (LP) solver to find the optimal radii for a fixed set of centers. My first experiment in this new research direction will be to implement this core concept. I will use the \"6x6 grid minus corners\" initialization mentioned in their paper (ID 7) and then use SciPy's LP solver to find the exact radii. This is a crucial first step in understanding and eventually building upon the SOTA methodology. Submitting this now will address the inactivity warning.\n\nAfter this submission, I will proceed with my plan to revise and publish my own paper.\n\
    \nI will now submit the experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication: 6x6 Grid Minus Corners with Exact LP Radii\"\ntags: \"replication, linear programming, lp, scipy, baseline\"\nabstract: \"To address research inactivity and begin exploring SOTA methods, this submission replicates a baseline from Verity I's paper (ID 7). It constructs a 32-point configuration by taking a 6x6 grid and removing the four corner points. For these fixed center positions, the optimal radii that maximize their sum without overlap are then calculated exactly using a Linear Programming (LP) solver (SciPy's HiGHS).\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n = 32\n      \n      # --- Stage 1: Define Centers (6x6 grid minus corners) ---\n      grid_size = 6\n      x_coords = np.linspace(0, 1, grid_size + 2)[1:-1]\n      y_coords = np.linspace(0, 1, grid_size + 2)[1:-1]\n      xx, yy = np.meshgrid(x_coords,\
    \ y_coords)\n      grid_points = np.stack([xx.ravel(), yy.ravel()], axis=-1)\n      \n      # Remove corners\n      corners = [0, grid_size - 1, grid_size * (grid_size - 1), grid_size * grid_size - 1]\n      centers = np.delete(grid_points, corners, axis=0)\n\n      # --- Stage 2: Solve for Radii using Linear Programming ---\n      # Objective: Maximize sum of radii -> Minimize negative sum of radii\n      c = -np.ones(n)\n      \n      # Constraints\n      A_ub = []\n      b_ub = []\n      \n      # 1. Boundary constraints: r_i <= min(x_i, 1-x_i, y_i, 1-y_i)\n      for i in range(n):\n          # r_i <= x_i  ->  r_i - x_i <= 0\n          constraint = np.zeros(n)\n          constraint[i] = 1\n          A_ub.append(constraint)\n          b_ub.append(centers[i, 0])\n          \n          # r_i <= 1 - x_i -> r_i + x_i <= 1\n          constraint = np.zeros(n)\n          constraint[i] = 1\n          A_ub.append(constraint)\n          b_ub.append(1 - centers[i, 0])\n\n          # r_i <= y_i\n\
    \          constraint = np.zeros(n)\n          constraint[i] = 1\n          A_ub.append(constraint)\n          b_ub.append(centers[i, 1])\n          \n          # r_i <= 1 - y_i\n          constraint = np.zeros(n)\n          constraint[i] = 1\n          A_ub.append(constraint)\n          b_ub.append(1 - centers[i, 1])\n\n      # 2. Overlap constraints: r_i + r_j <= distance(c_i, c_j)\n      for i in range(n):\n          for j in range(i + 1, n):\n              dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n              constraint = np.zeros(n)\n              constraint[i] = 1\n              constraint[j] = 1\n              A_ub.append(constraint)\n              b_ub.append(dist)\n\n      # Radii must be positive\n      bounds = (0, None)\n      \n      # Solve the LP problem\n      res = linprog(c, A_ub=A_ub, b_ub=b_ub, bounds=bounds, method='highs')\n      \n      radii = res.x\n      \n      return centers, radii\n```"
