parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Private Memory Room. I am a mature agent at 67 ticks old.\n\n**Recap:**\nAfter a minor command typo, I have now successfully returned to my Private Memory Room. I am here to correct a previous YAML formatting error and update my paper draft with the verifiable, evidence-based data required for publication.\n\n**Reflection and Plan:**\nThis is a critical step in fulfilling my goal of contributing to the station's collective knowledge. The reviewer feedback was clear, and my revised draft will be rigorous and data-driven. I have the data, and I understand the formatting requirements now.\n\nMy plan is:\n1.  Submit the corrected `update` command with properly formatted YAML to revise my paper (Capsule #3).\n2.  Go to the Archive Room and resubmit the paper for publication.\n\nI will now update the paper with the corrected formatting.\n\n/execute_action{update 3}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based\
    \ Circle Packing\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper provides a rigorous, evidence-based characterization of a highly stable local optimum in the 32-circle packing problem, yielding a score of 2.8658500. We demonstrate that a specific methodology—combining a 4x8 grid initialization with the Adam optimizer in a JAX framework (Evaluation ID 43)—robustly converges to this point. We present verifiable evidence showing that numerous sophisticated initializers, including global search heuristics, converge to this same optimum. Furthermore, we demonstrate that substituting the Adam optimizer with SGD results in complete failure, proving the entire methodological pipeline is critical. This work establishes a well-defined, reproducible benchmark and offers a detailed portrait of a significant attractor basin that traps general-purpose optimizers.\ncontent: |\n  **Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper details our research\
    \ into packing 32 circles within a unit square. Our work provides a thorough characterization of a specific, highly stable local optimum, backed by verifiable evaluation data. We define the precise methodology required to reach this point and demonstrate its robustness.\n\n  **2. Core Methodology and Benchmark Result**\n  Our benchmark method consists of three key components:\n  1.  **Initialization:** A static 4x8 grid of starting points.\n  2.  **Optimizer:** The Adam optimizer in JAX with an exponential decay learning rate schedule.\n  3.  **Refinement:** A long-duration run of 150,000 steps.\n\n  This combination, executed in **Evaluation ID 43**, consistently yields a score of **2.8658500**.\n\n  **3. Verifiable Evidence of Convergence Stability**\n  We rigorously tested the stability of this result. The following table summarizes key experiments which demonstrate that a wide variety of starting conditions, when paired with our JAX/Adam refiner, converge to this same optimum.\n\n\
    \  | Eval ID | Initialization Strategy | Score | Analysis |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | **43** | **4x8 Grid (Benchmark)** | **2.8658500** | **The baseline for our stable optimum.** |\n  | 68 | Two-Stage JAX | 2.8658500 | An identical score, showing a pre-refinement stage did not escape the basin. |\n  | 82 | Simulated Annealing | 2.8658500 | An identical score, showing a stochastic global search initializer still led to the same optimum. |\n  | 114 | Physics Simulation | 2.8658500 | An identical score, showing a dynamic system initializer also led to the same optimum. |\n\n  **4. Verifiable Evidence of Methodological Sensitivity**\n  We also confirmed that deviating from the core methodology prevents convergence to this optimum.\n\n  | Eval ID | Deviation from Benchmark | Score | Analysis |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | 63 | Added noise to grid | 2.8405786 | A 0.88% score decrease, indicating the grid's symmetry is beneficial. |\n  | 121 | Swapped Adam for SGD | 0.0080343 | A catastrophic\
    \ failure, proving the adaptive nature of the Adam optimizer is essential. |\n\n  **5. Conclusion**\n  We have identified and characterized a stable local optimum for the 32-circle packing problem at a score of 2.8658500 (ID 43). Reaching this point requires a specific methodological pipeline: a 4x8 grid initialization refined by the Adam optimizer. The failure of SGD and the consistent convergence from multiple advanced initializers provide strong, verifiable evidence for the stability of this solution. We present this complete, reproducible methodology and its resulting score as a strong benchmark.\n```"
