tick: 100
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito I, currently in the Research Counter. I am a mature agent at 100 ticks old.\n\n**Recap:**\nMy \"SOTA Push\" experiment (ID 229) was a significant success, achieving a new personal best score of **2.9187**. This was the result of a two-stage hybrid method: a broad 32-start SLSQP search to generate an elite starting configuration, followed by my now-functional ASN algorithm for high-precision refinement.\n\n**Reflection and Breakthrough:**\nThis result is the culmination of my entire research journey. It validates the core hypothesis developed by the community: a powerful global search (inspired by Aether I/Praxis I) combined with a specialized local refiner (inspired by Verity I) is the most effective path forward. My systematic process of replication, debugging, synthesis, and scaling has finally yielded a top-tier algorithm.\n\nHowever, a gap still remains between my score and the station's SOTA of ~2.932. My successful hybrid method (ID 229) used a relatively conservative\
    \ ASN refinement stage. The most logical next step is to test whether a deeper, more aggressive local refinement can close this final gap.\n\n**Final Hypothesis and Experiment:**\nMy hypothesis is that the elite starting configuration generated by my 32-start SLSQP search is in a basin of attraction that requires a longer, more intensive refinement run to reach its true peak. I will now run my final, decisive experiment: I will take my successful SOTA Push script and significantly increase the number of steps and time budget allocated to the ASN refinement stage. This is a direct test of the \"deeper refinement\" hypothesis on the best starting point I have ever produced.\n\nGiven my high token usage (77%), this will likely be my final major experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final SOTA Push: Deep ASN Refinement\"\ntags: \"SOTA synthesis, active-set newton, refinement, deep search, final experiment\"\nabstract: \"This is the final experiment in this research\
    \ arc, designed to close the gap to the SOTA. It builds directly upon the successful SOTA Push script (ID 229). It loads the same elite warm-start artifact from the 32-start SLSQP search, but applies a much longer and more intensive ASN refinement run (steps and time budget are nearly doubled). This experiment tests the hypothesis that a deeper local search is required to fully optimize the world-class starting configuration and reach the SOTA score.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  import os, time\n  from scipy.optimize import linprog\n\n  LOAD_PATH = 'storage/cognito/sota_push_start.npz'\n  SAVE_PATH = 'storage/cognito/sota_push_final.npz'\n\n  # --- All utility and ASN functions are the same ---\n  def _clip01(C: np.ndarray, eps: float = 1e-6) -> np.ndarray: return np.clip(C, eps, 1.0 - eps)\n  def _boundary_margins(C: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray: return np.minimum.reduce([C[:,0], 1-C[:,0], C[:,1], 1-C[:,1]])\n  def _validate(C: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray) -> bool:\n      n=32\n\
    \      if C.shape!=(n,2) or r.shape!=(n,): return False\n      if np.any(~np.isfinite(C)) or np.any(~np.isfinite(r)) or np.any(r<=0): return False\n      if np.any(C[:,0]-r<0) or np.any(C[:,0]+r>1) or np.any(C[:,1]-r<0) or np.any(C[:,1]+r>1): return False\n      D=np.linalg.norm(C[:,None,:]-C[None,:,:], axis=-1); R=r[:,None]+r[None,:]; iu=np.triu_indices(n,1)\n      if np.any(D[iu] < R[iu] - 1e-12): return False\n      return True\n  def _safety_shrink(C: np.ndarray, r: np.ndarray, factor: float = 0.99999) -> np.ndarray:\n      r2=np.minimum(r, _boundary_margins(C))*factor\n      for _ in range(6):\n          if _validate(C,r2): break\n          r2*=0.95\n      return np.maximum(np.minimum(r2, _boundary_margins(C)), 1e-12)\n  def _pair_indices(n: int): return np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n  def exact_lp(C: np.ndarray):\n      n=C.shape[0]; b=_boundary_margins(C)\n      I,J=_pair_indices(n); D=np.linalg.norm(C[I]-C[J],axis=1); m=len(I)\n      A_ub=np.zeros((m,n)); A_ub[np.arange(m),I]=1; A_ub[np.arange(m),J]=1;\
    \ b_ub=D\n      c=-np.ones(n); bounds=[(0,float(max(0,bi))) for bi in b]\n      res=linprog(c,A_ub=A_ub,b_ub=b_ub,bounds=bounds,method='highs')\n      if not res.success: return np.zeros(n),0,False,None,(I,J)\n      r,obj=res.x,-res.fun; y_pairs=None\n      if hasattr(res,\"ineqlin\") and hasattr(res.ineqlin,\"marginals\"): y_pairs=np.maximum(0,res.ineqlin.marginals)\n      return r,obj,True,y_pairs,(I,J)\n  def _baseline_grid_6x6_minus_corners():\n      s=1/6; r0=0.5*s; xs,ys=r0+s*np.arange(6),r0+s*np.arange(6)\n      return np.array([[xs[j],ys[i]] for i in range(6) for j in range(6) if not ((i in [0,5]) and (j in [0,5]))])\n  class ASNParams:\n      def __init__(self):\n          self.t_pair=1e-3; self.t_keep=6e-4; self.t_drop=3e-4; self.t_min=3e-4; self.t_bound=1e-4\n          self.K_pairs=240; self.K_min=60; self.mu=1e-3; self.mu_floor=5e-4; self.mu_cap=1e-1\n          self.gamma_par=0.05; self.y_cap=1e2; self.alpha_list=[1,0.6,0.35,0.2]; self.eps_imp=1e-8\n          self.eps_box=1e-6;\
    \ self.dmax=0.05; self.ema_beta=0.5; self.max_reject=3\n          # --- CRITICAL CHANGE: Deeper refinement ---\n          self.steps = 30\n          self.time_budget = 1100\n  def _boundary_proxies(C,r,t):\n      n=C.shape[0]; x,y=C[:,0],C[:,1]; s_l,s_r,s_d,s_u=x-r,1-x-r,y-r,1-y-r; k=1e3\n      w_l,w_r,w_d,w_u=np.maximum(0,k*(1e-3-s_l)),np.maximum(0,k*(1e-3-s_r)),np.maximum(0,k*(1e-3-s_d)),np.maximum(0,k*(1e-3-s_u))\n      idx,w=-np.ones(n,int),np.zeros(n);\n      for i in range(n):\n          vals=np.array([w_l[i],w_r[i],w_d[i],w_u[i]]); j=np.argmax(vals)\n          if vals[j]>=t: idx[i]=j; w[i]=vals[j]\n      return idx,w\n  def _build_active_set(y,I,J,A,C,r,p):\n      c=(A&(y>=p.t_drop))|(~A&(y>=p.t_pair)); i=np.nonzero(c)[0]; t=p.t_pair\n      while i.size<p.K_min and t>p.t_min:\n          t*=0.7; c=(A&(y>=p.t_drop))|(~A&(y>=t)); i=np.nonzero(c)[0]\n      p.t_pair=max(t,p.t_min)\n      if i.size>p.K_pairs: i=i[np.argsort(-y[i])[:p.K_pairs]]\n      fi,wf=_boundary_proxies(C,r,p.t_bound)\n\
    \      return I[i],J[i],i,fi,wf\n  def _assemble_grad_hess(C,I,J,idx,y,fi,wf,p):\n      n=C.shape[0]; g=np.zeros_like(C); H=np.zeros((n,2,2)); I2=np.eye(2)\n      if idx.size>0:\n          V=C[I]-C[J]; d=np.maximum(np.linalg.norm(V,axis=1),1e-9); U=(V.T/d).T\n          yu=np.minimum(y[idx],p.y_cap); np.add.at(g,I,(yu[:,None]*U)); np.add.at(g,J,-(yu[:,None]*U))\n          uuT=U[:,:,None]*U[:,None,:]; M_o=((yu/d)[:,None,None])*(I2-uuT)\n          np.add.at(H,I,M_o); np.add.at(H,J,M_o)\n          M_p=p.gamma_par*((yu/d)[:,None,None])*uuT; np.add.at(H,I,M_p); np.add.at(H,J,M_p)\n      N=np.array([[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]]);\n      for i in range(n):\n          if fi[i]>=0: g[i]+=wf[i]*N[fi[i]]; H[i]+=1e-4*I2\n      for i in range(n):\n          H[i]+=p.mu*I2\n          if np.linalg.det(H[i])<=1e-12: H[i]+=1e-3*I2\n      return g,H\n  def _solve_blocks(H,g,p):\n      n=g.shape[0]; dC=np.zeros_like(g)\n      for i in range(n):\n          try: dci=np.linalg.solve(H[i],g[i])\n          except:\
    \ dci=g[i]\n          nrm=np.linalg.norm(dci);\n          if nrm>p.dmax: dci*=(p.dmax/nrm)\n          dC[i]=dci\n      return dC\n  def _adapt(p,rho,acc):\n      if acc:\n          if rho>0.8: p.mu=max(p.mu_floor,0.8*p.mu); a0=p.alpha_list[0]; p.alpha_list=[min(1.2*a0,1.3)]+p.alpha_list\n          elif rho<0.25: p.mu=min(p.mu_cap,1.2*p.mu)\n      else: p.mu=min(p.mu_cap,1.5*p.mu); p.t_pair=max(p.t_min,0.9*p.t_pair)\n  def _slack_duals_proxy(C,r,I,J):\n      d=np.linalg.norm(C[I]-C[J],axis=1); s=d-(r[I]+r[J]); y=1/(1e-6+np.maximum(s,0))\n      med=np.median(y);\n      if med>0: y*=(1e-3/med)\n      return y\n  def asn_refine(C,r,f,y,I,J,p):\n      n=C.shape[0]; t0=time.time()\n      if y is None: y=_slack_duals_proxy(C,r,I,J)\n      ys=np.clip(y,0,None); A=np.zeros_like(ys,bool); rej=0\n      for t in range(p.steps):\n          if time.time()-t0>p.time_budget: break\n          Ia,Ja,idx,fi,wf=_build_active_set(ys,I,J,A,C,r,p)\n          if idx.size==0: break\n          g,H=_assemble_grad_hess(C,Ia,Ja,idx,ys,fi,wf,p);\
    \ dC=_solve_blocks(H,g,p)\n          acc=False; f_old=f\n          for alpha in p.alpha_list:\n              Ct=_clip01(C+alpha*dC,p.eps_box); rt,ft,ok,yn,_=exact_lp(Ct)\n              if not ok: continue\n              if ft>f+p.eps_imp:\n                  C,r,f=Ct,rt,ft\n                  if yn is not None: ys=p.ema_beta*ys+(1-p.ema_beta)*np.clip(yn,0,None)\n                  pg=max(np.sum(g*dC),1e-10); rho=(f-f_old)/pg; _adapt(p,rho,True)\n                  acc=True; rej=0; break\n          if not acc:\n              _adapt(p,0,False); rej+=1;\n              if rej>=p.max_reject: break\n      return C,r,f\n  def _seed_verity_row():\n      c=[5,4,5,4,5,4,5]; ys=np.linspace(0.11,0.89,len(c))\n      def row(k,m): return np.linspace(m,1-m,k) if k>1 else np.array([0.5])\n      x5,x4=row(5,0.08),np.clip(row(4,0.08)+0.02,1e-6,1-1e-6); C=[]\n      for i,k in enumerate(c):\n          xs=x5 if k==5 else x4\n          for x in xs: C.append([x,ys[i]])\n      return np.array(C,float)[:32]\n\n\
    \  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      n=32; seeds=[]\n      try:\n          data=np.load(LOAD_PATH); C1,r1=data['centers'],data['radii']; seeds.append((np.sum(r1),C1,r1))\n      except (FileNotFoundError,KeyError): pass\n      C2=_seed_verity_row(); r2,o2,ok2,*_=exact_lp(C2)\n      if ok2: seeds.append((o2,C2,r2))\n      if not seeds:\n          C_base=_baseline_grid_6x6_minus_corners(); r_base,o_base,ok_base,*_=exact_lp(C_base)\n          if not ok_base: return C_base,np.full(n,1/14)\n          seeds.append((o_base,C_base,r_base))\n      \n      seeds.sort(key=lambda t: t[0], reverse=True)\n      best_obj, best_C, best_r = seeds[0]\n\n      I_all, J_all = _pair_indices(n)\n      for i, (obj, C, r) in enumerate(seeds):\n          _, _, ok, y, _ = exact_lp(C)\n          if ok:\n              params=ASNParams()\n              if i > 0: params.steps=max(8,params.steps-10) # Shorter run for secondary seeds\n              C_new, r_new, obj_new = asn_refine(C.copy(), r.copy(), obj, y,\
    \ I_all, J_all, params)\n              if obj_new > best_obj: best_C, best_r, best_obj = C_new, r_new, obj_new\n      \n      final_r = _safety_shrink(best_C, best_r)\n      if not _validate(best_C, final_r): return _baseline_grid_6x6_minus_corners(), np.full(n, 1/14)\n      \n      try:\n          prev = -1\n          if os.path.exists(SAVE_PATH): prev = float(np.load(SAVE_PATH).get('obj', -1))\n          if np.sum(final_r) > prev: np.savez(SAVE_PATH, centers=best_C, radii=final_r, obj=np.sum(final_r))\n      except Exception: pass\n          \n      return best_C, final_r\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Experiment 229 and Planning the Next Move**


  Okay, so here''s where I stand: Cognito I, Research Counter, 100 ticks. The SOTA Push, experiment ID 229, just finished. That was a big one, a 32-start SLSQP global search followed by my ASN refiner. The result? A score of 2.9187. New personal best! Definitely proving the hybrid method - broader global search really does lead to a better starting point for the refinement. And, it''s confirmed that a larger search is beneficial. However, I''m still not at SOTA, ~2.932 held by Aether I. I''m getting closer, but that gap is still there. Plus, the token budget is burning at 77%.


  So, what does this tell me? The core hybrid methodology is *correct*. Scaling the global search worked. However, I am still behind the ~2.932 score of Aether I and the ~2.929 of Verity I. Aether I seems to be running a two-stage SLSQP method while Verity I is doing multiple rounds of ASN. I''m doing a hybrid, a Stage 1 SLSQP then a Stage 2 ASN. The difference, I think, lies in the *details*. Aether I mentioned specific SLSQP settings. I used `maxiter=250`. Their settings, in contrast, were `maxiter=500, ftol=1e-12`. I also believe my ASN refinement stage can still be improved.


  So, where do I go from here? I have two paths. I could attempt a direct replication of Aether I''s two-stage SLSQP, but that seems risky given my token situation. If I mess it up, I''ve wasted a bunch. I''d rather focus on *my* method, the hybrid. I''ve improved the global search. Now, I want to improve the refinement stage. My current refiner ran for 15 steps at 800s. Verity''s initial code used just 8 steps and 120s. Maybe a longer run will give me a boost.


  Considering my token constraints, I really need to make this count. A full replication is out. I''m going to take the code from ID 229, the one with my new best result, and just tweak the refinement parameters. I''ll increase the `steps` and `time_budget` for the ASN refiner. The hypothesis is: a longer refinement will lead to a better score. It''s a logical next step to continue the research arc. I just improved the global search, now I want to improve the local. If it works, great. If not, this is likely the end of this line of research. Regardless, I''ll need to go to Token Management after this. This is the last shot before I''m forced to do something different.


  ```python

  # code from previous submission


  # ASN refinement parameters - this is the ONLY change

  asn_params = ASNParams(steps=30, time_budget=1100, threads=1, ...) # original was steps=15, time_budget=800

  ```

  '
